What Should We Actually Make of the Data Center Numbers?
 2026-09-06
 Harrison Chamberlain

 
Below are prepared remarks written for a town planning commission weighing a large data-center proposal. They're framed as spoken testimony rather than an essay, because the point of the piece is to model how someone might actually walk a room full of concerned residents through this reasoning in real time — not to summarize it after the fact

Testimony: 
My name is Harrison Chamberlain, and I'm a student studying economics. I don't represent the developer or any resource groups. I believe this community benefits from a conversation about the costs and benefits of this new development.
You may have seen the headlines. "The new data center will consume 800,000 gallons of water every day." "A massive facility will use as much electricity as an entire city." Those numbers are real. But a number by itself doesn't tell you whether something is a problem. It just tells you that something is big.
For example, The University of Rochester, an institution most of us think of as a good neighbor, uses about 225 million kilowatt-hours of electricity a year, 250 million gallons of water, and holds a permit to discharge up to 45 million gallons a day into the Genesee River. If I read you those numbers cold, without telling you it was the University, you'd think I was describing an environmental disaster. You'd be wrong, because I left out the only question that actually matters: compared to what?
So let me tell you what I think this commission should actually be asking.
First: is this project paying full market price for the water and electricity it uses? If it is, then it's not "taking" anything from the rest of us — it's competing for resources the same way every other business does, and the price system is doing its job.
Second and this is the real question: is anyone bearing a cost that isn't being priced or compensated? Tens of thousands of daily commuter trips generate real particulate pollution, concentrated along specific roads and in specific neighborhoods. That burden doesn't show up in the developer's utility bill. It lands on people who live near the route, whether or not they see a single job or tax dollar from this project. That's a legitimate economic problem not because the project is "big," but because the costs and the benefits are landing on different people.
Third: what happens if we say no? The water and electricity this project would use don't just evaporate; they get allocated somewhere else, sometimes to a less regulated, less accountable operation in another state that will happily take the investment we turned away. Restricting development isn't automatically the safe choice, any more than approving it automatically is.
I'm not asking you to wave this project through, and I'm not asking you to block it. I'm asking you to stop debating the size of the numbers and start debating whether the costs are priced, whether they're falling on the people who consent to them, and whether our permitting and monitoring can actually catch it if they're not.
The conversation we should be having is if the costs outway the benefits. We should focus on whether or not the new development is more or less beneficial than what would take its place.
Thank you.


